WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:02.119
You update a record.

00:02.129 --> 00:04.621
The old value is gone.

00:05.780 --> 00:07.669
SirixDB keeps every version.

00:07.679 --> 00:11.235
Each commit writes only what changed and

00:11.245 --> 00:12.366
shares the rest,

00:12.376 --> 00:15.027
so storage grows with the change,

00:15.037 --> 00:16.411
not the history.

00:17.190 --> 00:19.172
Any revision opens directly:

00:19.182 --> 00:22.324
by number, by timestamp,

00:22.334 --> 00:24.464
or bitemporally.

00:24.474 --> 00:26.843
Nothing to replay:

00:26.853 --> 00:30.124
revision one reads like the latest.

00:31.290 --> 00:35.363
Every field keeps its identity across revisions.

00:35.373 --> 00:37.964
Follow one value through time,

00:37.974 --> 00:40.336
or diff any two revisions:

00:40.346 --> 00:43.074
exact inserts, updates and deletes.

00:43.860 --> 00:45.209
And it's bitemporal:

00:45.219 --> 00:48.721
SirixDB knows when a fact was recorded

00:48.731 --> 00:50.501
and when it was true,

00:50.511 --> 00:54.112
so a correction never erases what you knew.

00:55.450 --> 00:57.462
Its indexes are versioned too,

00:57.472 --> 01:02.572
including columnar projections for fast analytics on any revision.

01:04.080 --> 01:06.871
Embed it as a Java library or

01:06.881 --> 01:08.185
natively in C,

01:08.195 --> 01:09.881
C++, Rust and Python,

01:09.891 --> 01:12.534
run the REST server,

01:12.544 --> 01:13.848
use the shell,

01:13.858 --> 01:17.580
or let AI agents edit safely over MCP.

01:19.020 --> 01:19.578
SirixDB.

01:19.588 --> 01:22.283
Every version of your data,

01:22.293 --> 01:26.537
kept and queryable as fast as the present.

01:26.547 --> 01:29.234
Append-only, bitemporal, embeddable,

01:29.244 --> 01:30.658
and open source.
